(window.THART=window.THART||{})[131]={"h": "<section class=\"arch-c\" id=\"a-c1\" data-t=\"Operating note\"><div class=\"archd\"><span class=\"arnum\">1</span><div class=\"arkick\">Chapter one</div><h2>Operating note</h2></div><p class=\"arintro\">The case on one page.</p><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s1-1\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">1.1</span><h3>The case at a glance</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><div class=\"artw\"><table class=\"artb\"><caption>Myringoplasty and tympanoplasty</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\"></th><th scope=\"col\">Summary</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">Indications</th><td>A perforation that restricts water activities; recurrent discharge or recurrent ear infections; to improve hearing</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Contraindications</th><td>An only hearing ear (prefer a hearing aid unless active disease persists despite maximal treatment); active vestibular disease; active discharge not yet settled (dry the ear first)</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Anaesthesia</th><td>General for most; intravenous sedation with local anaesthetic for a limited, transcanal repair in a cooperative patient</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Position</th><td>Supine, bed turned 180 degrees with the feet toward the anaesthetist, head turned away</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Antibiotics</th><td>Not routinely needed; a single dose of cefazolin (clindamycin if allergic) for an infected ear or when a prosthesis is placed</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Monitoring</th><td>Facial nerve monitoring optional; not the standard of care for this operation</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Imaging</th><td>CT only if cholesteatoma or mastoid disease is suspected; MRI for imbalance, vertigo or asymmetric sensorineural loss</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Tray</th><td>Middle ear instrument set; round knives, picks, cup forceps, Freer elevator; Lempert speculum; high-speed drill with cutting and diamond burs for canaloplasty or mastoidectomy; ossicular prostheses if reconstruction is planned; Gelfoam, Silastic sheet</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Before theatre</th><td>Dry the ear with drops or systemic antibiotics; read every previous operation note (local graft tissue may be used up); confirm the patient will come back for follow-up</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s1-2\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">1.2</span><h3>Consent</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><div class=\"arbox k-consent\"><h5>What to tell the patient</h5><ul><li><b>What it does:</b> closes the hole in the eardrum, and checks and repairs the middle ear behind it, aiming for a dry ear that can get wet and, if possible, better hearing.</li><li><b>The graft may not take</b>, leaving or creating a hole that needs another operation; more likely after previous surgery or with a poorly working Eustachian tube.</li><li><b>Hearing may not improve</b>, may get worse, and very rarely is lost completely.</li><li><b>Taste change</b> on the side of the tongue if the taste nerve is stretched or cut; usually temporary.</li><li><b>Dizziness</b>, usually short-lived; <b>tinnitus</b>.</li><li><b>Facial weakness:</b> very rare.</li><li><b>Numbness of the top of the ear</b> after an incision behind it.</li><li><b>Late problems:</b> the drum healing away from the bone (lateralisation), scarring in the front angle (blunting), and small skin cysts.</li><li><b>Water precautions</b> for several weeks to months.</li></ul></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s1-3\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">1.3</span><h3>Before you start</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b1-3-1\"><h4><i>1.3.1</i>The history</h4><ul class=\"arl\"><li><b>Hearing loss:</b> sudden or gradual.</li><li><b>Discharge:</b> chronic or recurrent.</li><li><b>Trauma</b>, such as a slap, a cotton bud, barotrauma, diving or forceful syringing.</li><li><b>Pain</b> is not typical of a simple perforation: look for another cause in the head and neck.</li><li><b>Vertigo</b> must be investigated before surgery; do not operate on an ear with an active vestibular problem.</li><li>Previous ear surgery and grommets; <b>cleft palate</b> repair (tube dysfunction); severe seasonal allergy; anticoagulants; smoking.</li></ul></div><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b1-3-2\"><h4><i>1.3.2</i>The examination</h4><p>Use the microscope.</p><ul class=\"arl\"><li><b>The perforation:</b> size (small, subtotal, total); site (central or <b>marginal</b>); a \"trail\" of keratin running from a marginal edge means squamous epithelium in the middle ear.</li><li>Wet or dry; the middle ear mucosa; cholesteatoma, hypertrophic mucosa or granulation suggests a mastoidectomy may also be needed.</li><li><b>The other ear:</b> retraction, atelectasis, effusion and tympanosclerosis point to poor tube function, which predicts a poorer result, especially in children.</li></ul></div><div class=\"arbr\" id=\"a-b1-3-3\"><h4><i>1.3.3</i>The audiogram</h4><p>A small perforation can have normal hearing, and a near-total one causes about <b>35 to 40 dB</b> of conductive loss. A bigger gap than the hole explains points to an <b>ossicular</b> break or fixation, which you must plan for. In mixed loss, closing the gap may still leave the patient needing a hearing aid; but if a severe mixed loss is too much for a comfortable aid, correcting the conductive part can help rehabilitation.</p><div class=\"arbox k-flag\"><h5>Who is less likely to succeed</h5><p>Multiple failed previous repairs, persistent Eustachian tube dysfunction, severe disease in the other ear, and palatal problems. Tell these patients beforehand, and consider cartilage.</p></div></div></div></details></section><section class=\"arch-c\" id=\"a-c2\" data-t=\"Anatomy you must see\"><div class=\"archd\"><span class=\"arnum\">2</span><div class=\"arkick\">Chapter two</div><h2>Anatomy you must see</h2></div><p class=\"arintro\">The drum, the canal and what lies behind.</p><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s2-1\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">2.1</span><h3>The tympanic membrane</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><ul class=\"arl\"><li><b>Pars tensa</b> and <b>pars flaccida</b>; three layers: outer squamous epithelium, middle fibrous layer, inner mucosa.</li><li>The <b>fibrous annulus</b> sits in the bony <b>sulcus</b> of the tympanic ring.</li><li><b>Central</b> perforations have a rim of drum all round; <b>marginal</b> ones reach the annulus.</li></ul></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s2-2\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">2.2</span><h3>The canal</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><ul class=\"arl\"><li>The <b>bony-cartilaginous junction</b> is where the canal block is injected.</li><li>The <b>tympanomastoid</b> and <b>tympanosquamous suture lines</b> guide the vascular strip incisions.</li><li>The <b>anterior canal bulge</b> often hides the anterior annulus; it must be drilled for lateral grafting.</li><li>The <b>linea temporalis</b> marks the upper limit of the periosteal flap; the <b>temporomandibular joint</b> lies in front of the anterior canal wall.</li></ul></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s2-3\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">2.3</span><h3>The middle ear</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><ul class=\"arl\"><li><b>Ossicles:</b> malleus (head, neck, handle, lateral and anterior processes), incus (body, short and long processes), stapes (head, crura, footplate).</li><li>The <b>chorda tympani</b> crosses medial to the drum, near the posterior annulus, under the tympanomeatal flap.</li><li>The <b>facial nerve:</b> its tympanic segment above the oval window and its mastoid segment behind the posterior canal wall.</li><li><b>Tympanosclerosis:</b> chalky plaques in the drum, on the ossicles and the medial wall; remove plaque that stiffens the drum.</li></ul></div></details></section><section class=\"arch-c\" id=\"a-c3\" data-t=\"The operation: myringoplasty\"><div class=\"archd\"><span class=\"arnum\">3</span><div class=\"arkick\">Chapter three</div><h2>The operation: myringoplasty</h2></div><p class=\"arintro\">Small and traumatic perforations.</p><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s3-1\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">3.1</span><h3>Anaesthesia of the canal</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Inject 1% to 2% lidocaine with 1:100,000 adrenaline in four quadrants, just lateral to the bony-cartilaginous junction, through a Lempert speculum, until the skin blanches. Inject behind the ear too if you will take fascia or use a postauricular approach.</p><div class=\"arbox k-see\"><h5>What you should see</h5><ul><li>The canal skin blanching all round, down to the annulus, without a bleb lifting the skin.</li></ul></div><div class=\"arbox k-danger\"><h5>Blebs and the facial nerve</h5><p>Injecting fast raises blisters that tear the flap you are about to lift. Anaesthetic that tracks deep to the stylomastoid foramen causes a temporary facial palsy; warn the patient and wait.</p></div><div class=\"arbox k-senior\"><h5>Senior&#x27;s pearl</h5><p>Inject slowly and wait ten minutes. A dry canal is made at this step, not later with adrenaline pledgets.</p></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s3-2\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">3.2</span><h3>The fresh traumatic tear</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Fold the edges out from the medial surface so no squamous epithelium is left inside, then lay them back together. Support them with Gelfoam soaked in ciprofloxacin (with or without dexamethasone) medially, and a cigarette paper, Steri-Strip or silk patch on the outer surface.</p><div class=\"arbox k-see\"><h5>What you should see</h5><ul><li>Everted edges lying flat and touching, with no skin curled into the middle ear and the handle of the malleus in its normal position.</li></ul></div><div class=\"arbox k-danger\"><h5>Vertigo, sensorineural loss or facial weakness</h5><p>These mean the injury went deeper than the drum (stapes subluxation, perilymph fistula): this is no longer a clinic patch.</p></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s3-3\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">3.3</span><h3>The small chronic perforation</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Touch the edges with phenol or trichloroacetic acid, then <b>freshen the margin all round with a sharp pick</b>, removing epithelium that has grown under the medial surface and making the edge bleed; then patch the outer surface.</p><div class=\"arbox k-danger\"><h5>Skin left inside</h5><p>Squamous epithelium left on the medial surface of the edge, or trapped under any graft, grows into a cholesteatoma. Rimming the perforation properly is the single most important step of every technique.</p></div><div class=\"arbox k-see\"><h5>What you should see</h5><ul><li>A bleeding raw rim all the way round, and healthy, dry middle ear mucosa through the hole.</li></ul></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s3-4\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">3.4</span><h3>Fat or areolar tissue graft</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>If patching fails, prepare the edges the same way, fill the middle ear behind the hole with Gelfoam, and pass a dumbbell of fat from the ear lobe through the perforation, or place compressed areolar tissue or fascia medial to it, held by Gelfoam on both sides.</p><div class=\"arbox k-see\"><h5>What you should see</h5><ul><li>The fat plug bulging on both sides of the drum like a collar stud, filling the hole with no gap at its edge.</li></ul></div><div class=\"arbox k-senior\"><h5>Senior&#x27;s pearl</h5><p>Fat plugs suit small central perforations, under about a quarter of the drum, in a dry ear. A larger hole needs a formal graft.</p></div></div></details></section><section class=\"arch-c\" id=\"a-c4\" data-t=\"The operation: cartilage butterfly (inlay)\"><div class=\"archd\"><span class=\"arnum\">4</span><div class=\"arkick\">Chapter four</div><h2>The operation: cartilage butterfly (inlay)</h2></div><p class=\"arintro\">Eavey's butterfly graft suits small to medium perforations whose <b>whole edge can be seen through the canal</b>. With no canal incisions and no packing, there is less pain, the hearing improves at once, and the operation is short. (Because no tympanomeatal flap is raised, some call it a myringoplasty.)</p><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s4-1\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">4.1</span><h3>Rim and measure</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Inject the canal and the tragus. Rim the perforation with a curved pick, without enlarging it beyond what you can see through the canal. Measure it with a right-angled hook or round knife.</p><figure class=\"arfig armf\" id=\"a-f1\"><div class=\"one\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"arimg\" data-fig=\"m/131_6\" data-set=\"thm131\" aria-label=\"Enlarge figure 1\"><img src=\"fig2/m/131_6.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></button></div><figcaption><b>Figure 1</b>Measuring the perforation with a right-angled hook; the exposed malleus and tympanosclerosis help lock the graft.<cite>Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 131.6</cite></figcaption></figure><div class=\"arbox k-see\"><h5>What you should see</h5><ul><li>A freshened rim with no epithelium turned in, and the whole margin of the perforation visible through the canal: if the anterior edge is hidden, this technique is the wrong one.</li></ul></div><div class=\"arbox k-danger\"><h5>Enlarging the hole</h5><p>Do not tear the remnant while rimming; the cartilage is cut to the measurement you take now.</p></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s4-2\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">4.2</span><h3>Harvest and shape the cartilage</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Incise the back (posteromedial) surface of the tragus through skin and cartilage in one stroke with a size 15 blade, keeping the dome of the tragus for cosmesis; free the cartilage, keeping perichondrium on one or both sides, and close with one or two plain gut stitches. Cut the cartilage to the <b>exact shape</b> of the perforation but <b>1 to 2 mm larger</b>. With a blade, cut a groove about <b>2 mm deep</b> all round its edge, keeping the two flanges equal, so it opens like butterfly wings.</p><figure class=\"arfig armf\" id=\"a-f2\"><div class=\"one\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"arimg\" data-fig=\"m/131_7\" data-set=\"thm131\" aria-label=\"Enlarge figure 2\"><img src=\"fig2/m/131_7.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></button></div><figcaption><b>Figure 2</b>The tragal cartilage shaped 1 to 2 mm larger than the perforation, with a groove cut all round its edge so it opens like butterfly wings.<cite>Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 131.7</cite></figcaption></figure><div class=\"arbox k-see\"><h5>What you should see</h5><ul><li>A disc 1 to 2 mm larger than the hole with an even groove round its edge and perichondrium intact on the lateral face.</li></ul></div><div class=\"arbox k-danger\"><h5>An uneven groove</h5><p>If one flange is thinner than the other, the graft tilts or falls into the middle ear. Recut it if the wings are unequal.</p></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s4-3\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">4.3</span><h3>Snap it in</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Pass the graft down the canal and seat it so one flange lies <b>medial</b> to the edge of the perforation and the other, with perichondrium, <b>lateral</b>. Check every point of the rim, then press gently on the centre: the whole drum should move with it.</p><figure class=\"arfig armf\" id=\"a-f3\"><div class=\"one\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"arimg\" data-fig=\"m/131_8\" data-set=\"thm131\" aria-label=\"Enlarge figure 3\"><img src=\"fig2/m/131_8.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></button></div><figcaption><b>Figure 3</b>The butterfly graft in place, one edge lifted to show the two flanges.<cite>Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 131.8</cite></figcaption></figure><div class=\"arbox k-see\"><h5>What you should see</h5><ul><li>A graft that sits flush in the hole all round, with the medial flange under the drum remnant at every point.</li></ul></div><div class=\"arbox k-senior\"><h5>Senior&#x27;s pearl</h5><p>Sizing makes or breaks this graft: too large and it will not snap in, too small and it falls into the middle ear. Use the same instrument to measure the hole and to mark the cartilage.</p></div></div></details></section><section class=\"arch-c\" id=\"a-c5\" data-t=\"The operation: lateral (overlay) graft\"><div class=\"archd\"><span class=\"arnum\">5</span><div class=\"arkick\">Chapter five</div><h2>The operation: lateral (overlay) graft</h2></div><p class=\"arintro\">The lateral graft suits <b>total and near-total perforations</b>, revisions, and drums thick with plaque or scarred remnants that need removing.</p><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s5-1\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">5.1</span><h3>Vascular strip and postauricular approach</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Under the microscope, rinse the canal and make <b>vascular strip incisions</b> along the tympanomastoid and tympanosquamous suture lines (a semilunar incision joining them just lateral to the annulus is optional; leaving it out lets the strip lift the outer layer of the drum remnant with it). Make a postauricular incision a few millimetres behind the crease, ending just below the lobule and <b>never beyond the mastoid tip</b>, where the facial nerve becomes superficial in children. Dissect forward in the loose areolar plane over the temporalis fascia until the posterior canal skin is a few millimetres thick, and place a self-retaining retractor.</p><figure class=\"arfig armf\" id=\"a-f4\"><div class=\"one\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"arimg\" data-fig=\"m/131_9\" data-set=\"thm131\" aria-label=\"Enlarge figure 4\"><img src=\"fig2/m/131_9.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></button></div><figcaption><b>Figure 4</b>The vascular strip incisions along the tympanomastoid and tympanosquamous suture lines.<cite>Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 131.9</cite></figcaption></figure><div class=\"arbox k-see\"><h5>What you should see</h5><ul><li>The loose areolar plane over the temporalis fascia, the posterior canal skin thinned from behind, and the spine of Henle at the back of the meatus.</li></ul></div><div class=\"arbox k-danger\"><h5>The facial nerve in children</h5><p>Under about two years the mastoid tip is undeveloped and the nerve leaves the skull superficially. Keep the incision high and off the tip.</p></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s5-2\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">5.2</span><h3>Harvest the fascia</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Incise the true temporalis fascia (not the muscle) for 2 cm at its lower end, lift it off the muscle with a Freer, and cut a 2 by 2 cm piece; the loose areolar layer over it can be used if it is thick enough. Clean off muscle and fat on a block, press it flat and let it dry under a lamp. Make a 7- or T-shaped periosteal incision along the linea temporalis and down the skin incision, and lift the periosteal flap into the retractor with the ear.</p><div class=\"arbox k-see\"><h5>What you should see</h5><ul><li>True temporalis fascia: white and glistening with the red muscle visible beneath once it is lifted.</li><li>A flat, dry, parchment-like graft with no fat or muscle on it.</li></ul></div><div class=\"arbox k-senior\"><h5>Senior&#x27;s pearl</h5><p>Take more than you think you need, and take it first: it dries while you work, and a dry graft handles like paper; a wet one curls.</p></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s5-3\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">5.3</span><h3>Lift the canal skin</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Complete the elevation of the vascular strip and fold it out of the canal into the retractor. With a large round knife, lift the rest of the canal skin and periosteum from the annulus <b>outward</b> to the bony-cartilaginous junction, leaving it attached there and folding it out. If the outer layer of the drum remnant stays behind, remove it separately.</p><figure class=\"arfig armf\" id=\"a-f5\"><div class=\"one\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"arimg\" data-fig=\"m/131_10\" data-set=\"thm131\" aria-label=\"Enlarge figure 5\"><img src=\"fig2/m/131_10.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></button></div><figcaption><b>Figure 5</b>The periosteum and canal skin lifted from the annulus outward, toward the cartilaginous canal.<cite>Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 131.10</cite></figcaption></figure><div class=\"arbox k-see\"><h5>What you should see</h5><ul><li>Bare bone of the canal all round with the tympanosquamous and tympanomastoid sutures, and the canal skin hinged laterally in one piece.</li></ul></div><div class=\"arbox k-danger\"><h5>Tearing the skin</h5><p>The anterior canal skin is thin and stuck at the suture lines; divide the fibres there sharply. A torn or lost flap leaves bare bone that granulates and stenoses.</p></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s5-4\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">5.4</span><h3>Drill the canal</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Widen the bony canal with a size 3 cutting bur then a size 2 diamond with continuous suction irrigation, removing the <b>anterior and inferior bulges</b> until you can see the <b>whole annulus</b> at once and the anterior angle is opened. Keep away from the facial nerve posteriorly and the temporomandibular joint anteriorly.</p><figure class=\"arfig armf\" id=\"a-f6\"><div class=\"one\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"arimg\" data-fig=\"m/131_11\" data-set=\"thm131\" aria-label=\"Enlarge figure 6\"><img src=\"fig2/m/131_11.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></button></div><figcaption><b>Figure 6</b>Drilling away the anterior and inferior canal bulges with suction irrigation.<cite>Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 131.11</cite></figcaption></figure><div class=\"arbox k-danger\"><h5>Blunting</h5><p>A canal left narrow, or a graft that rides up onto the anterior or inferior canal wall instead of lying on the annulus, scars across the anterior angle, which blunts and thickens the drum and costs hearing. Drill until the whole annulus is in view, and keep the graft off the bone.</p></div><div class=\"arbox k-see\"><h5>What you should see</h5><ul><li>The whole annulus in one view of the microscope, including the anterior angle, without moving the patient's head.</li></ul></div><div class=\"arbox k-senior\"><h5>Senior&#x27;s pearl</h5><p>Leave a thin shell over the temporomandibular joint: if the bone turns pink or blue, stop. Keep the skin in the retractor moist while you drill.</p></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s5-5\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">5.5</span><h3>De-epithelialise the remnant</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Starting in front with a round knife along the bony annulus, scrape the squamous layer off the drum remnant all round, leaving the annulus and fibrous layer intact, and clear the rim of the perforation with cup forceps. For a total perforation or a thin graft, fill the middle ear with small Gelfoam pledgets.</p><div class=\"arbox k-danger\"><h5>Pearls and cholesteatoma</h5><p>Any squamous epithelium left under a lateral graft becomes an intratympanic cyst or cholesteatoma. Take your time over this step.</p></div><div class=\"arbox k-see\"><h5>What you should see</h5><ul><li>A dull, matt fibrous layer all round with no shiny epithelium, and the bare bony annulus anteriorly.</li></ul></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s5-6\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">5.6</span><h3>Place the graft</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Trim the dried fascia to an oval about 1.3 by 1.5 cm and cut a slit at 12 o'clock. Wet it, and lay it <b>lateral</b> to the annulus and the cleaned remnant, with the slit tucked <b>medial to the malleus handle</b> (in contact with the tensor tendon, in Sheehy's description). Overlap the two edges of the slit, or fold the anterior flap back over the handle. Smooth the graft onto the annulus in front and below, <b>keeping it off the bony canal</b>, and bring any excess onto the posterior wall. If the malleus is missing, cut two slits to make a tongue that tucks under the scutum into the attic, then overlap the side flaps over it.</p><figure class=\"arfig armf\" id=\"a-f7\"><div class=\"one\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"arimg\" data-fig=\"m/131_12\" data-set=\"thm131\" aria-label=\"Enlarge figure 7\"><img src=\"fig2/m/131_12.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></button></div><figcaption><b>Figure 7</b>The dried fascia trimmed to an oval with a slit at 12 o'clock for the malleus handle.<cite>Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 131.12</cite></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"arfig armf\" id=\"a-f8\"><div class=\"one\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"arimg\" data-fig=\"m/131_13\" data-set=\"thm131\" aria-label=\"Enlarge figure 8\"><img src=\"fig2/m/131_13.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></button></div><figcaption><b>Figure 8</b>The graft lateral to the annulus with its slit medial to the handle.<cite>Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 131.13</cite></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"arfig armf\" id=\"a-f9\"><div class=\"one\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"arimg\" data-fig=\"m/131_14\" data-set=\"thm131\" aria-label=\"Enlarge figure 9\"><img src=\"fig2/m/131_14.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></button></div><figcaption><b>Figure 9</b>The anterior flap folded back over the exposed handle, or both flaps overlapped leaving only the umbo exposed.<cite>Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 131.14</cite></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"arfig armf\" id=\"a-f10\"><div class=\"one\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"arimg\" data-fig=\"m/131_15\" data-set=\"thm131\" aria-label=\"Enlarge figure 10\"><img src=\"fig2/m/131_15.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></button></div><figcaption><b>Figure 10</b>With no malleus, two cuts create a tongue to tuck into the attic.<cite>Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 131.15</cite></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"arfig armf\" id=\"a-f11\"><div class=\"one\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"arimg\" data-fig=\"m/131_16\" data-set=\"thm131\" aria-label=\"Enlarge figure 11\"><img src=\"fig2/m/131_16.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></button></div><figcaption><b>Figure 11</b>The tongue tucked into the attic and the flaps overlapped to seal the top of the graft.<cite>Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 131.16</cite></figcaption></figure><div class=\"arbox k-danger\"><h5>Lateralisation</h5><p>A graft not placed medial to the malleus handle drifts outward from the ossicles over 6 to 12 months, and the hearing falls. The handle anchors the graft.</p></div><div class=\"arbox k-see\"><h5>What you should see</h5><ul><li>The graft flat on the remnant and annulus, tucked under the malleus handle, with its anterior edge stopping at the annulus and not climbing the canal wall.</li></ul></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s5-7\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">5.7</span><h3>Replace the skin and pack</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Lay the canal skin back on the bone, overlapping the graft by about 1 mm, without folding its edges under (folded edges form canal cysts). Roll a dry cigar of Gelfoam into the anterior sulcus, or place a piece of thick Silastic over the graft, then Gelfoam on top. Release the retractors, return the vascular strip, close the periosteal flap with one stitch to steady the ear, check through the speculum that the strip is in place with its edges everted, fill the canal with Gelfoam and place a cotton ball. Close the periosteum and skin in layers with absorbable sutures and glue, and apply a mastoid dressing for 24 hours.</p><figure class=\"arfig armf\" id=\"a-f12\"><div class=\"one\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"arimg\" data-fig=\"m/131_17\" data-set=\"thm131\" aria-label=\"Enlarge figure 12\"><img src=\"fig2/m/131_17.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></button></div><figcaption><b>Figure 12</b>The canal skin returned over the bone, overlapping the fascia by about 1 mm.<cite>Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 131.17</cite></figcaption></figure><div class=\"arbox k-see\"><h5>What you should see</h5><ul><li>The graft flat on the annulus all round, medial to the handle, clear of the anterior canal wall, and the canal skin lying flat with no edges turned under.</li></ul></div></div></details></section><section class=\"arch-c\" id=\"a-c6\" data-t=\"The operation: medial (underlay) graft\"><div class=\"archd\"><span class=\"arnum\">6</span><div class=\"arkick\">Chapter six</div><h2>The operation: medial (underlay) graft</h2></div><p class=\"arintro\">The medial graft suits most central perforations with an adequate rim, infrequent discharge and little middle ear disease. If the whole perforation is visible through the canal, the operation can be transcanal, with a small postauricular incision just for the fascia.</p><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s6-1\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">6.1</span><h3>Flap and middle ear</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Inject, rim the perforation, make the vascular strip incisions and join them with a <b>semilunar incision about 4 mm lateral to the annulus</b>. Harvest fascia as above. Lift the <b>tympanomeatal flap</b> with the annulus out of its sulcus with a round knife until you enter the middle ear, finding and preserving the <b>chorda tympani</b>. Inspect and feel the ossicles: if the chain is stiff, separate the incudostapedial joint to tell whether the stapes or the malleus and incus are fixed. Clear granulation, hyperplastic mucosa, cholesteatoma or plaque that limits movement.</p><div class=\"arbox k-see\"><h5>What you should see</h5><ul><li>The annulus lifting out of its sulcus, the chorda tympani crossing under the flap, then the incudostapedial joint, the round window niche and the promontory.</li><li>A round window reflex when you move the malleus: the chain is intact and mobile.</li></ul></div><div class=\"arbox k-danger\"><h5>The chorda and the incus</h5><p>Enter the middle ear posteroinferiorly, under the annulus, and work upward; entering high tears the chorda and can dislocate the incus.</p></div><div class=\"arbox k-senior\"><h5>Senior&#x27;s pearl</h5><p>Always test the chain before you graft. A perfect drum over a fixed or broken chain does not hear.</p></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s6-2\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">6.2</span><h3>Place the graft</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Fill the middle ear with Gelfoam as a bed, wet the graft, and place it against the <b>medial</b> surface of the perforation, tucked under the remnant all round. Lay the flap back and hold everything with Gelfoam lateral to the drum.</p><figure class=\"arfig armf\" id=\"a-f13\"><div class=\"one\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"arimg\" data-fig=\"m/131_18\" data-set=\"thm131\" aria-label=\"Enlarge figure 13\"><img src=\"fig2/m/131_18.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></button></div><figcaption><b>Figure 13</b>The medial graft: after freshening the edges and raising the tympanomeatal flap, the graft is placed medial to the perforation on a bed of Gelfoam.<cite>Myers, Operative Otolaryngology, fig. 131.18</cite></figcaption></figure><div class=\"arbox k-see\"><h5>What you should see</h5><ul><li>Graft visible behind the whole rim with no gap, especially anteriorly, once the flap is laid back.</li></ul></div><div class=\"arbox k-danger\"><h5>The anterior edge</h5><p>A medial graft fails where it has least support: anteriorly. If the anterior remnant is narrow, pull the graft through a small anterior canal-skin tunnel or pack the anterior mesotympanum firmly with Gelfoam.</p></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s6-3\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">6.3</span><h3>The over-under graft</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>When the perforation reaches the malleus handle, scrape the periosteum and any drum remnant off the handle (incise its periosteum with a sickle knife and peel from the umbo up to the lateral process), then place the graft <b>lateral to (\"over\") the handle</b> and <b>medial to (\"under\") the annulus and remnant</b>. Put Gelfoam over the handle to press the graft onto the bone.</p><div class=\"arbox k-see\"><h5>What you should see</h5><ul><li>A graft lying under the remnant all round, supported by Gelfoam, with the flap returned exactly and the chorda intact.</li></ul></div><div class=\"arbox k-senior\"><h5>Senior&#x27;s pearl</h5><p>Underlay grafts fail anteriorly, where the remnant is thin and the canal bulge hides the edge. The over-under technique, a little more Gelfoam anteriorly, or a cartilage graft for a large or high-risk anterior perforation, all reduce this.</p></div></div></details></section><section class=\"arch-c\" id=\"a-c7\" data-t=\"When it goes wrong\"><div class=\"archd\"><span class=\"arnum\">7</span><div class=\"arkick\">Chapter seven</div><h2>When it goes wrong</h2></div><p class=\"arintro\">Drills.</p><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s7-1\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">7.1</span><h3>Crisis drills</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><div class=\"arpath\"><h5>The face is weak in recovery</h5><ol><li><span class=\"n\">1</span><div><b>First</b><ul><li><span>wait for the local anaesthetic to wear off before acting</span></li></ul></div></li><li><span class=\"n\">2</span><div><b>If still weak</b><ul><li><span>remove the packing</span></li><li><span>examine the nerve's course</span></li><li><span>steroids</span></li><li><span>imaging and exploration if no improvement or the nerve was not seen</span></li></ul></div></li></ol></div><div class=\"arpath\"><h5>The canal is narrowing after lateral grafting</h5><ol><li><span class=\"n\">1</span><div><b>Recognise</b><ul><li><span>soft-tissue stenosis across the mid canal, sometimes looking like lateralisation</span></li></ul></div></li><li><span class=\"n\">2</span><div><b>Act</b><ul><li><span>early debridement and stenting in clinic</span></li><li><span>silver nitrate to granulation</span></li><li><span>keep or replace the Silastic stent</span></li></ul></div></li></ol></div><div class=\"arpath\"><h5>A small white cyst appears on the graft or canal</h5><ol><li><span class=\"n\">1</span><div><b>Recognise</b><ul><li><span>an epithelial pearl from trapped or folded-in skin</span></li></ul></div></li><li><span class=\"n\">2</span><div><b>Act</b><ul><li><span>open and marsupialise it in clinic while small</span></li></ul></div></li></ol></div><div class=\"arpath\"><h5>The hearing drops months after a lateral graft</h5><ol><li><span class=\"n\">1</span><div><b>Suspect</b><ul><li><span>lateralisation (graft lifted off the malleus) or anterior blunting</span></li></ul></div></li><li><span class=\"n\">2</span><div><b>Act</b><ul><li><span>microscopy and audiogram</span></li><li><span>revision surgery if hearing is significantly affected</span></li></ul></div></li></ol></div></div></details></section><section class=\"arch-c\" id=\"a-c8\" data-t=\"Aftercare\"><div class=\"archd\"><span class=\"arnum\">8</span><div class=\"arkick\">Chapter eight</div><h2>Aftercare</h2></div><p class=\"arintro\">Weeks of patience.</p><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s8-1\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">8.1</span><h3>After the operation</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><ul class=\"arl\"><li><b>Paper patch:</b> keep the ear dry and review until the patch migrates onto the canal wall, usually 6 to 12 weeks.</li><li><b>Packed myringoplasty:</b> remove the packing at 7 to 10 days.</li><li><b>Cartilage butterfly:</b> no drops needed; review at about 3 weeks; keep dry until healed, usually 3 to 4 weeks.</li><li><b>Lateral and medial grafts:</b> remove the mastoid dressing the next day; the wound can get wet after 24 hours with glue, but keep the canal dry with a Vaseline-coated cotton ball. Start drops twice a day a week before the review at about 3 weeks; they soften the Gelfoam so it can be suctioned out.</li><li><b>Silastic after lateral grafting</b> can be removed at the first review, or left as a stent for 6 to 8 weeks if there is concern about stenosis, blunting or lateralisation.</li><li>The drum should be intact by <b>3 weeks</b>; check mobility with pneumatic otoscopy and do tuning forks; <b>audiogram at 2 to 3 months</b>.</li><li><b>Water restrictions lift</b> at about 2 months after medial grafting and 3 to 4 months after lateral grafting.</li></ul></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s8-2\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">8.2</span><h3>Complications</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><div class=\"artw\"><table class=\"artb\"><caption>Complications of myringoplasty and tympanoplasty</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Complication</th><th scope=\"col\">Cause or prevention</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Graft failure, persistent perforation</td><td>Poor rimming; tube dysfunction; previous surgery; mucosal disease</td></tr><tr><td>Lateralisation</td><td>Lateral graft not placed medial to the malleus; 6 to 12 months later</td></tr><tr><td>Anterior blunting</td><td>Narrow canal or graft on the anterior canal wall</td></tr><tr><td>Epithelial pearls and cholesteatoma</td><td>Skin left medial to the graft or folded under</td></tr><tr><td>Canal stenosis</td><td>Healing of the canal after lateral grafting</td></tr><tr><td>Taste disturbance</td><td>Chorda tympani stretched or cut</td></tr><tr><td>Sensorineural loss, vertigo, tinnitus</td><td>Manipulation of the ossicles, drilling on the chain</td></tr><tr><td>Facial weakness</td><td>Local anaesthetic (wait), or injury</td></tr><tr><td>Wound infection</td><td>Uncommon</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s8-3\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">8.3</span><h3>Choosing a technique and material</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><div class=\"artw\"><table class=\"artb\"><caption>Techniques compared</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Technique</th><th scope=\"col\">Best for</th><th scope=\"col\">Watch for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Paper patch</td><td>Small fresh traumatic or small chronic perforation</td><td>Repeated application may be needed</td></tr><tr><td>Fat plug</td><td>Small central perforation after a failed patch</td><td>Small holes only</td></tr><tr><td>Cartilage butterfly</td><td>Small to medium perforations visible through the canal</td><td>Exact sizing</td></tr><tr><td>Medial (underlay)</td><td>Most central perforations with a rim</td><td>Anterior failure, narrowed middle ear space</td></tr><tr><td>Lateral (overlay)</td><td>Total and subtotal, revisions, diseased remnants</td><td>Blunting, lateralisation, pearls; harder to learn</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Success exceeds 90% with any technique in skilled hands, and some studies give 97% or more for button, overlay and underlay grafts alike; one large series slightly favoured lateral grafts. Graft materials give similar hearing, but cartilage gives a better-looking, more durable drum and is preferred for <b>high-risk ears</b> (atelectasis, cholesteatoma, revisions). Routine mastoidectomy with tympanoplasty has not clearly improved closure, though it may reduce revisions; add it for severe mucosal disease or cholesteatoma.</p></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s8-4\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">8.4</span><h3>Alternatives</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><p>Observation, or a hearing aid with water precautions, for any patient. A conductive loss over 30 to 35 dB usually limits function enough that observation alone is not reasonable, though an aid avoids surgical risk at the cost of batteries, breakdowns, background noise and feedback. A staged operation with Silastic in the middle ear may be needed to rebuild an aerated space first.</p></div></details></section><section class=\"arch-c\" id=\"a-c9\" data-t=\"For the examination\"><div class=\"archd\"><span class=\"arnum\">9</span><div class=\"arkick\">Chapter nine</div><h2>For the examination</h2></div><p class=\"arintro\">What the examiner wants to hear.</p><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s9-1\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">9.1</span><h3>Where the books differ</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><div class=\"arbox k-differ\"><h5>Where the books differ</h5><div class=\"cols\"><div><b>Dhingra</b>separates <b>myringoplasty</b> (closing the drum without inspecting the middle ear) from <b>tympanoplasty</b> (clearing disease and rebuilding the hearing mechanism) and gives the Wullstein types; lists contraindications to myringoplasty (discharge, uncontrolled nasal allergy, otitis externa, squamous ingrowth, an only hearing ear, and young age); compares underlay grafting (narrower middle ear, anterior reperforation) with overlay (blunting, pearls, lateralisation); and uses a paper patch with cautery of the rim, or a fat graft, for small holes.</div><div><b>Lee</b>covers tympanoplasty with ossicular reconstruction, noting that more patients reach a gap under 20 dB with a partial than a total prosthesis, and that graft material matters less than technique.</div></div><p>Myers describes three techniques in operative detail: the cartilage butterfly with exact sizing, Sheehy's lateral graft with vascular strip, canaloplasty and de-epithelialisation, and the medial graft with the over-under modification; it stresses rimming the perforation as the essential step in all of them, and the specific timetable for drops, reviews and lifting water restrictions.</p></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s9-2\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">9.2</span><h3>Viva essentials</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><div class=\"arbox k-exam\"><h5>What the examiner is looking for</h5><ul><li><b>Myringoplasty versus tympanoplasty</b>, and the <b>Wullstein</b> types.</li><li>Why a <b>gap larger than the hole</b> explains means ossicular disease.</li><li><b>Rimming</b> the perforation, and why.</li><li><b>Underlay versus overlay</b>: indications and each one's complications.</li><li><b>Lateralisation</b> and <b>blunting</b>: causes and prevention.</li><li>The <b>cartilage butterfly</b>: indications and the importance of sizing.</li><li>Graft materials and when to choose <b>cartilage</b>.</li><li><b>Prognostic factors</b>: the other ear, tube function, previous surgery, palate.</li></ul></div></div></details><details class=\"arsub\" id=\"a-s9-3\" open><summary><span class=\"arsn\">9.3</span><h3>Common pitfalls</h3><svg class=\"arch\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M6 9l6 6 6-6\"/></svg></summary><div class=\"arbody\"><div class=\"arbox k-pit\"><h5>Common pitfalls</h5><ul><li>Inadequate rimming.</li><li>Not checking the other ear and tube function.</li><li>Missing an ossicular problem the audiogram predicted.</li><li>A narrow canal or a graft on the anterior canal wall.</li><li>Leaving skin under the graft or folding canal skin edges under.</li><li>A lateral graft not medial to the malleus handle.</li><li>Extending the postauricular incision below the mastoid tip in a child.</li><li>Wrongly sized cartilage.</li></ul></div></div></details></section>", "stand": "Myringoplasty repairs the eardrum; tympanoplasty repairs the drum and also deals with the middle ear behind it, whether that is chronic infection, cholesteatoma or a broken ossicular chain, which usually means lifting a tympanomeatal flap. Both aim at the same three things: a dry ear, an intact drum (so the patient can get it wet again), and hearing kept or improved. Every technique, inlay, underlay or overlay, succeeds in more than nine out of ten ears in skilled hands; what decides the result is the surgeon's precision and the choice of patient. This chapter takes you from the assessment of a perforation, through paper patching, fat and cartilage butterfly grafts, to the lateral (overlay) and medial (underlay) techniques, and the complications each one invites.", "ch": ["Operating note", "Anatomy you must see", "The operation: myringoplasty", "The operation: cartilage butterfly (inlay)", "The operation: lateral (overlay) graft", "The operation: medial (underlay) graft", "When it goes wrong", "Aftercare", "For the examination"], "w": 4492, "n": 31, "st": [{"c": "myringoplasty", "t": "Anaesthesia of the canal", "d": "Inject 1% to 2% lidocaine with 1:100,000 adrenaline in four quadrants, just lateral to the bony-cartilaginous junction, through a Lempert speculum, until the skin blanches. Inject behind the ear too if you will take fascia or use a postauricular approach.", "f": [], "see": "The canal skin blanching all round, down to the annulus, without a bleb lifting the skin.", "risk": "Injecting fast raises blisters that tear the flap you are about to lift. Anaesthetic that tracks deep to the stylomastoid foramen causes a temporary facial palsy; warn the patient and wait.", "pearl": "Inject slowly and wait ten minutes. A dry canal is made at this step, not later with adrenaline pledgets.", "id": "a-s3-1"}, {"c": "myringoplasty", "t": "The fresh traumatic tear", "d": "Fold the edges out from the medial surface so no squamous epithelium is left inside, then lay them back together. Support them with Gelfoam soaked in ciprofloxacin (with or without dexamethasone) medially, and a cigarette paper, Steri-Strip or silk patch on the outer surface.", "f": [], "see": "Everted edges lying flat and touching, with no skin curled into the middle ear and the handle of the malleus in its normal position.", "risk": "These mean the injury went deeper than the drum (stapes subluxation, perilymph fistula): this is no longer a clinic patch.", "pearl": "", "id": "a-s3-2"}, {"c": "myringoplasty", "t": "The small chronic perforation", "d": "Touch the edges with phenol or trichloroacetic acid, then freshen the margin all round with a sharp pick, removing epithelium that has grown under the medial surface and making the edge bleed; then patch the outer surface.", "f": [], "see": "A bleeding raw rim all the way round, and healthy, dry middle ear mucosa through the hole.", "risk": "Squamous epithelium left on the medial surface of the edge, or trapped under any graft, grows into a cholesteatoma. Rimming the perforation properly is the single most important step of every technique.", "pearl": "", "id": "a-s3-3"}, {"c": "myringoplasty", "t": "Fat or areolar tissue graft", "d": "If patching fails, prepare the edges the same way, fill the middle ear behind the hole with Gelfoam, and pass a dumbbell of fat from the ear lobe through the perforation, or place compressed areolar tissue or fascia medial to it, held by Gelfoam on both sides.", "f": [], "see": "The fat plug bulging on both sides of the drum like a collar stud, filling the hole with no gap at its edge.", "risk": "", "pearl": "Fat plugs suit small central perforations, under about a quarter of the drum, in a dry ear. A larger hole needs a formal graft.", "id": "a-s3-4"}, {"c": "cartilage butterfly (inlay)", "t": "Rim and measure", "d": "Inject the canal and the tragus. Rim the perforation with a curved pick, without enlarging it beyond what you can see through the canal. Measure it with a right-angled hook or round knife.", "f": [6], "see": "A freshened rim with no epithelium turned in, and the whole margin of the perforation visible through the canal: if the anterior edge is hidden, this technique is the wrong one.", "risk": "Do not tear the remnant while rimming; the cartilage is cut to the measurement you take now.", "pearl": "", "id": "a-s4-1"}, {"c": "cartilage butterfly (inlay)", "t": "Harvest and shape the cartilage", "d": "Incise the back (posteromedial) surface of the tragus through skin and cartilage in one stroke with a size 15 blade, keeping the dome of the tragus for cosmesis; free the cartilage, keeping perichondrium on one or both sides, and close with one or two plain gut stitches. Cut the cartilage to the exact shape of the perforation but 1 to 2 mm larger. With a blade, cut a groove about 2 mm deep all round its edge, keeping the two flanges equal, so it opens like butterfly wings.", "f": [7], "see": "A disc 1 to 2 mm larger than the hole with an even groove round its edge and perichondrium intact on the lateral face.", "risk": "If one flange is thinner than the other, the graft tilts or falls into the middle ear. Recut it if the wings are unequal.", "pearl": "", "id": "a-s4-2"}, {"c": "cartilage butterfly (inlay)", "t": "Snap it in", "d": "Pass the graft down the canal and seat it so one flange lies medial to the edge of the perforation and the other, with perichondrium, lateral. Check every point of the rim, then press gently on the centre: the whole drum should move with it.", "f": [8], "see": "A graft that sits flush in the hole all round, with the medial flange under the drum remnant at every point.", "risk": "", "pearl": "Sizing makes or breaks this graft: too large and it will not snap in, too small and it falls into the middle ear. Use the same instrument to measure the hole and to mark the cartilage.", "id": "a-s4-3"}, {"c": "lateral (overlay) graft", "t": "Vascular strip and postauricular approach", "d": "Under the microscope, rinse the canal and make vascular strip incisions along the tympanomastoid and tympanosquamous suture lines (a semilunar incision joining them just lateral to the annulus is optional; leaving it out lets the strip lift the outer layer of the drum remnant with it). Make a postauricular incision a few millimetres behind the crease, ending just below the lobule and never beyond the mastoid tip, where the facial nerve becomes superficial in children. Dissect forward in the loose areolar plane over the temporalis fascia until the posterior canal skin is a few millimetres thick, and place a self-retaining retractor.", "f": [9], "see": "The loose areolar plane over the temporalis fascia, the posterior canal skin thinned from behind, and the spine of Henle at the back of the meatus.", "risk": "Under about two years the mastoid tip is undeveloped and the nerve leaves the skull superficially. Keep the incision high and off the tip.", "pearl": "", "id": "a-s5-1"}, {"c": "lateral (overlay) graft", "t": "Harvest the fascia", "d": "Incise the true temporalis fascia (not the muscle) for 2 cm at its lower end, lift it off the muscle with a Freer, and cut a 2 by 2 cm piece; the loose areolar layer over it can be used if it is thick enough. Clean off muscle and fat on a block, press it flat and let it dry under a lamp. Make a 7- or T-shaped periosteal incision along the linea temporalis and down the skin incision, and lift the periosteal flap into the retractor with the ear.", "f": [], "see": "True temporalis fascia: white and glistening with the red muscle visible beneath once it is lifted. A flat, dry, parchment-like graft with no fat or muscle on it.", "risk": "", "pearl": "Take more than you think you need, and take it first: it dries while you work, and a dry graft handles like paper; a wet one curls.", "id": "a-s5-2"}, {"c": "lateral (overlay) graft", "t": "Lift the canal skin", "d": "Complete the elevation of the vascular strip and fold it out of the canal into the retractor. With a large round knife, lift the rest of the canal skin and periosteum from the annulus outward to the bony-cartilaginous junction, leaving it attached there and folding it out. If the outer layer of the drum remnant stays behind, remove it separately.", "f": [10], "see": "Bare bone of the canal all round with the tympanosquamous and tympanomastoid sutures, and the canal skin hinged laterally in one piece.", "risk": "The anterior canal skin is thin and stuck at the suture lines; divide the fibres there sharply. A torn or lost flap leaves bare bone that granulates and stenoses.", "pearl": "", "id": "a-s5-3"}, {"c": "lateral (overlay) graft", "t": "Drill the canal", "d": "Widen the bony canal with a size 3 cutting bur then a size 2 diamond with continuous suction irrigation, removing the anterior and inferior bulges until you can see the whole annulus at once and the anterior angle is opened. Keep away from the facial nerve posteriorly and the temporomandibular joint anteriorly.", "f": [11], "see": "The whole annulus in one view of the microscope, including the anterior angle, without moving the patient's head.", "risk": "A canal left narrow, or a graft that rides up onto the anterior or inferior canal wall instead of lying on the annulus, scars across the anterior angle, which blunts and thickens the drum and costs hearing. Drill until the whole annulus is in view, and keep the graft off the bone.", "pearl": "Leave a thin shell over the temporomandibular joint: if the bone turns pink or blue, stop. Keep the skin in the retractor moist while you drill.", "id": "a-s5-4"}, {"c": "lateral (overlay) graft", "t": "De-epithelialise the remnant", "d": "Starting in front with a round knife along the bony annulus, scrape the squamous layer off the drum remnant all round, leaving the annulus and fibrous layer intact, and clear the rim of the perforation with cup forceps. For a total perforation or a thin graft, fill the middle ear with small Gelfoam pledgets.", "f": [], "see": "A dull, matt fibrous layer all round with no shiny epithelium, and the bare bony annulus anteriorly.", "risk": "Any squamous epithelium left under a lateral graft becomes an intratympanic cyst or cholesteatoma. Take your time over this step.", "pearl": "", "id": "a-s5-5"}, {"c": "lateral (overlay) graft", "t": "Place the graft", "d": "Trim the dried fascia to an oval about 1.3 by 1.5 cm and cut a slit at 12 o'clock. Wet it, and lay it lateral to the annulus and the cleaned remnant, with the slit tucked medial to the malleus handle (in contact with the tensor tendon, in Sheehy's description). Overlap the two edges of the slit, or fold the anterior flap back over the handle. Smooth the graft onto the annulus in front and below, keeping it off the bony canal, and bring any excess onto the posterior wall. If the malleus is missing, cut two slits to make a tongue that tucks under the scutum into the attic, then overlap the side flaps over it.", "f": [12, 13, 14, 15, 16], "see": "The graft flat on the remnant and annulus, tucked under the malleus handle, with its anterior edge stopping at the annulus and not climbing the canal wall.", "risk": "A graft not placed medial to the malleus handle drifts outward from the ossicles over 6 to 12 months, and the hearing falls. The handle anchors the graft.", "pearl": "", "id": "a-s5-6"}, {"c": "lateral (overlay) graft", "t": "Replace the skin and pack", "d": "Lay the canal skin back on the bone, overlapping the graft by about 1 mm, without folding its edges under (folded edges form canal cysts). Roll a dry cigar of Gelfoam into the anterior sulcus, or place a piece of thick Silastic over the graft, then Gelfoam on top. Release the retractors, return the vascular strip, close the periosteal flap with one stitch to steady the ear, check through the speculum that the strip is in place with its edges everted, fill the canal with Gelfoam and place a cotton ball. Close the periosteum and skin in layers with absorbable sutures and glue, and apply a mastoid dressing for 24 hours.", "f": [17], "see": "The graft flat on the annulus all round, medial to the handle, clear of the anterior canal wall, and the canal skin lying flat with no edges turned under.", "risk": "", "pearl": "", "id": "a-s5-7"}, {"c": "medial (underlay) graft", "t": "Flap and middle ear", "d": "Inject, rim the perforation, make the vascular strip incisions and join them with a semilunar incision about 4 mm lateral to the annulus. Harvest fascia as above. Lift the tympanomeatal flap with the annulus out of its sulcus with a round knife until you enter the middle ear, finding and preserving the chorda tympani. Inspect and feel the ossicles: if the chain is stiff, separate the incudostapedial joint to tell whether the stapes or the malleus and incus are fixed. Clear granulation, hyperplastic mucosa, cholesteatoma or plaque that limits movement.", "f": [], "see": "The annulus lifting out of its sulcus, the chorda tympani crossing under the flap, then the incudostapedial joint, the round window niche and the promontory. A round window reflex when you move the malleus: the chain is intact and mobile.", "risk": "Enter the middle ear posteroinferiorly, under the annulus, and work upward; entering high tears the chorda and can dislocate the incus.", "pearl": "Always test the chain before you graft. A perfect drum over a fixed or broken chain does not hear.", "id": "a-s6-1"}, {"c": "medial (underlay) graft", "t": "Place the graft", "d": "Fill the middle ear with Gelfoam as a bed, wet the graft, and place it against the medial surface of the perforation, tucked under the remnant all round. Lay the flap back and hold everything with Gelfoam lateral to the drum.", "f": [18], "see": "Graft visible behind the whole rim with no gap, especially anteriorly, once the flap is laid back.", "risk": "A medial graft fails where it has least support: anteriorly. If the anterior remnant is narrow, pull the graft through a small anterior canal-skin tunnel or pack the anterior mesotympanum firmly with Gelfoam.", "pearl": "", "id": "a-s6-2"}, {"c": "medial (underlay) graft", "t": "The over-under graft", "d": "When the perforation reaches the malleus handle, scrape the periosteum and any drum remnant off the handle (incise its periosteum with a sickle knife and peel from the umbo up to the lateral process), then place the graft lateral to (\"over\") the handle and medial to (\"under\") the annulus and remnant. Put Gelfoam over the handle to press the graft onto the bone.", "f": [], "see": "A graft lying under the remnant all round, supported by Gelfoam, with the flap returned exactly and the chorda intact.", "risk": "", "pearl": "Underlay grafts fail anteriorly, where the remnant is thin and the canal bulge hides the edge. The over-under technique, a little more Gelfoam anteriorly, or a cartilage graft for a large or high-risk anterior perforation, all reduce this.", "id": "a-s6-3"}]};
